1
00:00:20,855 --> 00:00:24,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five hindrances.

2
00:00:24,918 --> 00:00:39,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual desire, aversion, that lazy drowsiness, sloth, restless agitation and doubt.

3
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:44,811
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how to overcome those things?

4
00:00:44,831 --> 00:00:48,254
[SPEAKER_01]: How to... Well, I think the...

5
00:00:50,828 --> 00:00:56,732
[SPEAKER_01]: The first point to make is to clear up what those hindrances are not.

6
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:09,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I think the contemporary sort of Buddhist meditators take on the hindrances, it's actually quite... it's actually quite mistaken.

7
00:01:09,962 --> 00:01:17,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people would have a notion that, you know, hindrances are these things that sort of arise occasionally in me.

8
00:01:18,927 --> 00:01:22,630
[SPEAKER_01]: And, oh, and my mind is fully kind of focused on the lust or aversion.

9
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:24,492
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the hindrance of lust or aversion.

10
00:01:25,332 --> 00:01:25,573
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

11
00:01:25,593 --> 00:01:28,735
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the result of the hindrance of lust or aversion and so on.

12
00:01:29,496 --> 00:01:38,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Hindrances in themselves are pretty much on the, on the, like, on the level of your currently enduring existence as a whole.

13
00:01:38,603 --> 00:01:42,026
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as a basis for your world, it's a hindrance.

14
00:01:43,347 --> 00:01:44,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, only in jhāna,

15
00:01:45,175 --> 00:01:46,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is free from hindrances.

16
00:01:47,276 --> 00:01:53,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Only when the samādhi has been developed to the extent of jhāna, mind has overcome hindrances.

17
00:01:55,180 --> 00:02:04,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else, even your day-to-day life, even if you're not necessarily gripped by lust, aversion or doubt, you are within the domain of the hindrances.

18
00:02:06,146 --> 00:02:11,749
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're on the level pretty much of your existential situation.

19
00:02:11,769 --> 00:02:12,650
[SPEAKER_01]: And by that I mean,

20
00:02:13,673 --> 00:02:17,935
[SPEAKER_01]: At any given time, you are either lustful, or liable to lust.

21
00:02:18,795 --> 00:02:22,117
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either irritable, or liable to irritation.

22
00:02:22,857 --> 00:02:28,580
[SPEAKER_01]: You're either lazy, distracted, or liable to those things to manifest.

23
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:35,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Same goes with the agitation and worry, or liable to agitation and worry, and doubtful, or liable to doubt.

24
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:43,624
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, it's not that there's like these five hindrances, one, two, three, four, five.

25
00:02:45,306 --> 00:02:49,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, you're within the domain of the five as one thing.

26
00:02:50,490 --> 00:03:01,758
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you have the five aggregates, and then on account of the five aggregates, you have to discern, single out one of them at a time, if you... if that's what you discern and focus your attention on.

27
00:03:02,399 --> 00:03:03,980
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the hindrances is the same principle.

28
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:06,502
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the five hindrances is your basis.

29
00:03:07,296 --> 00:03:18,861
[SPEAKER_01]: The basis of an unenlightened, undeveloped mind is the domain of the five hindrances, and then, they are exemplified and manifested through one of them at a time, or the liability of one of them through the time.

30
00:03:20,601 --> 00:03:25,423
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why surmounting the hindrances equals surmounting the world, in the first jhāna.

31
00:03:28,505 --> 00:03:34,207
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's surmounting the entire existential situation, whereby, now, you are, eh...

32
00:03:35,946 --> 00:03:48,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And same goes for hate and all the other hindrances.

33
00:03:49,243 --> 00:03:53,488
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the reason why this is important is because for as long as people think hindrances are these things that come and go,

34
00:03:54,518 --> 00:03:58,240
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to really do to deal with them is just manage the state while it's there.

35
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:16,471
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually start understanding that, you know, hindrances are on the level of my situation already as it is, even if I don't discern any specific hindrance that's already within the hindrance, that will now force you to broaden the context more and more and more and more until you reach that supreme shore of safety from hindrances that's in the first jhāna.

36
00:04:17,752 --> 00:04:19,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because these things don't arise.

37
00:04:19,913 --> 00:04:21,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Buddha doesn't say, eh...

38
00:04:22,892 --> 00:04:26,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandoning the five hindrances, surmounting the joys and griefs in regard to the world.

39
00:04:26,855 --> 00:04:29,617
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say, joys and griefs doesn't arise in regard to the world.

40
00:04:29,637 --> 00:04:30,778
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it means you surmount it.

41
00:04:30,798 --> 00:04:31,499
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's there.

42
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:37,223
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of joys and griefs and five hindrances is there, but you have surmounted it.

43
00:04:37,263 --> 00:04:42,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, I have no issues, no hindrances arising for me, thus I'm entering the jhāna.

44
00:04:43,388 --> 00:04:45,730
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's well within the domain of the hindrances.

45
00:04:46,858 --> 00:05:00,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the five is really the one, the one gateway, as we always speak, through which these five different examples of exemplifications would then come and become more particularized.

46
00:05:00,241 --> 00:05:12,884
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the suttas talk about, you know, the temporary liberation from the hindrances, yeah, there are many different ways you can understand what temporary means, as in like, oh, this moment I'm hindrance, this moment I'm not hindrance, thus I'm temporarily free from hindrance.

47
00:05:13,584 --> 00:05:14,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you can also understand it as

48
00:05:15,687 --> 00:05:21,429
[SPEAKER_01]: You can surmount the domain of the five hindrances, regardless of which one is there for you the most.

49
00:05:21,889 --> 00:05:28,390
[SPEAKER_01]: But you surmount the gateway of the five, and you have surpassed them, you are free from them.

50
00:05:28,471 --> 00:05:36,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you become negligent, if you don't cultivate and protect that gateway, you will fall back into the domain of the hindrances.

51
00:05:36,453 --> 00:05:41,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you lost your temporary liberation, or your liberation was indeed temporary.

52
00:05:43,795 --> 00:05:44,635
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the gateway...

53
00:05:45,367 --> 00:05:46,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the gateway, the five, yeah.

54
00:05:46,908 --> 00:05:51,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's... you have to start thinking on the level of that gate, the peripheral context.

55
00:05:51,912 --> 00:05:58,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, the gateway of the five is why I get lust here, anger there, doubt yesterday, fear tomorrow.

56
00:05:59,597 --> 00:06:01,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because those things are the actual hindrances.

57
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:04,641
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just examples of that domain that you live in.

58
00:06:06,042 --> 00:06:09,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's often, like you would say, my domain, one way would be to reflect.

59
00:06:10,285 --> 00:06:12,347
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there, as the Buddha would say, is there a reason lust in me?

60
00:06:13,346 --> 00:06:15,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Can the non-arisen lust arise?

61
00:06:15,647 --> 00:06:20,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not just what's here right now, but what is not here right now, that could be right here right now.

62
00:06:22,088 --> 00:06:22,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Lust, anger.

63
00:06:23,209 --> 00:06:27,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that liability you start to include as a hindrance already.

64
00:06:27,750 --> 00:06:30,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I might not have any sensual desire now, but am I liable?

65
00:06:30,311 --> 00:06:30,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

66
00:06:30,831 --> 00:06:33,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, hindrance of sensuality is there.

67
00:06:33,932 --> 00:06:39,313
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a foolish thing to think that, oh, because it's not here right now, when I focus, I don't see any lust, I'm free from it.

68
00:06:41,088 --> 00:06:41,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you liable?

69
00:06:41,689 --> 00:06:41,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

70
00:06:42,249 --> 00:06:43,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Means the hindrance is there.

71
00:06:43,811 --> 00:06:45,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Liability is part of the hindrance.

72
00:06:45,974 --> 00:06:46,915
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I don't know.

73
00:06:48,016 --> 00:06:50,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, so is the hindrance of doubt and lack of clarity there.

74
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:51,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

75
00:06:51,821 --> 00:06:52,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

76
00:06:52,101 --> 00:06:55,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't know if I'm liable to lust.

77
00:06:57,027 --> 00:06:57,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I?

78
00:06:57,468 --> 00:06:57,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I not?

79
00:06:58,749 --> 00:07:00,031
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm liable to doubt.

80
00:07:01,254 --> 00:07:03,935
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know... Well, if you don't know you're liable to lust, means you are.

81
00:07:04,375 --> 00:07:08,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only time to not be liable to lust is to be clear about liability of the lust that you have abandoned.

82
00:07:09,077 --> 00:07:13,578
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're liable to one of those hindrances, then you're in the domain.

83
00:07:13,618 --> 00:07:13,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

84
00:07:14,199 --> 00:07:16,659
[SPEAKER_01]: One hindrance means the basis of the five is there.

85
00:07:16,679 --> 00:07:19,140
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's always the five hindrances when the Buddha speaks about.

86
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:21,981
[SPEAKER_01]: The five nivāraṇas, not three and a half or two.

87
00:07:22,902 --> 00:07:23,782
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always the five.

88
00:07:25,321 --> 00:07:31,143
[SPEAKER_01]: And these are the five ways those... the five would show itself to you, so to speak, and would harass you and overwhelm you.

89
00:07:31,703 --> 00:07:40,726
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why often, like, the arahant's liberation, the arahant's developing of mindfulness often goes heading to... he's the one who destroyed the hindrances, the mounter of the hindrances of the five.

90
00:07:41,568 --> 00:07:47,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the level of surmounting of the hindrances is the level of surmounting of the world, which is the basis for arahantship.

91
00:07:47,389 --> 00:07:56,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start thinking of them, instead of these individual things that occasionally come your way, but start seeing them as basically your world is within the five hindrances.

92
00:07:57,452 --> 00:07:59,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sense restraint is within the five hindrances.

93
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:03,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Your dhamma practice is within the five hindrances.

94
00:08:03,233 --> 00:08:06,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Only time to not be within the five hindrances is in the first jhāna.

95
00:08:08,211 --> 00:08:14,539
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the one who has samādhi entrances, then, dwells and abides in that viveka, that seclusion from unwholesome states.

96
00:08:15,099 --> 00:08:22,729
[SPEAKER_01]: And we just said, whether he walks, sits down, eats, whatever, goes to the toilet, everything is divine, everything is safe, because he's withdrawn from it.

97
00:08:24,718 --> 00:08:27,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the first step to withdraw yourself from it is to see them for what they are.

98
00:08:28,479 --> 00:08:35,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to think just managing occasional, like, irritation or doubt, a little bit of doubt, is I'm dealing with the hindrances.

99
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:38,681
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see it on the level of the context that you're already within that.

100
00:08:39,441 --> 00:08:41,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Liability to them means they're there.

101
00:08:41,762 --> 00:08:43,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be liable to them.

102
00:08:43,962 --> 00:08:53,064
[SPEAKER_01]: You start thinking like that, then you can get to, like, well, eventually, depending on, you know, how much effort you put in, how much clarity you develop, but you get to see that you are responsible.

103
00:08:54,482 --> 00:08:59,185
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not responsible for hindrances arising, but you're responsible for them hindering you.

104
00:09:00,987 --> 00:09:06,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the one that basically takes them up, ignorantly, one way or the other.

105
00:09:06,971 --> 00:09:09,292
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not on the level of the choice, as I say often.

106
00:09:09,332 --> 00:09:12,274
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you choose to take and now you just have to choose to not take it.

107
00:09:13,415 --> 00:09:20,000
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like sometimes you choose to do something that then results in you being unable to not take them.

108
00:09:23,929 --> 00:09:34,673
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop thinking of them individually, but see them as one, then doesn't matter what hindrance is there, your attitude towards the... the... any hindrance, that's where you start looking for.

109
00:09:35,153 --> 00:09:38,435
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, in lust I can't read with the subha, in anger I can't read with menta...

110
00:09:38,675 --> 00:09:42,556
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just managing these spontaneous little states that have arisen for me.

111
00:09:42,576 --> 00:09:51,560
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it doesn't matter whether it's lust, aversion, delusion, eh... distraction, fear, worry, unwholesome state is present, what is your mental attitude in regard to that?

112
00:09:53,312 --> 00:10:02,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a way, you could practice freedom from hindrances by practicing the discernment of liability to the unwholesome of any kind, no matter if it's five or more, whatever.

113
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:04,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what they all have in common, by the way.

114
00:10:06,222 --> 00:10:09,064
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why are you then liable to unwholesome states?

115
00:10:10,265 --> 00:10:13,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Why can't you just not be pressured by unwholesome states?

116
00:10:14,508 --> 00:10:15,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the answer is quite simple.

117
00:10:16,089 --> 00:10:18,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Because some of the unwholesome states you don't see as unwholesome.

118
00:10:19,072 --> 00:10:20,313
[SPEAKER_01]: And you, willingly,

119
00:10:21,097 --> 00:10:24,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Entertain them, delight in them, welcome them, act out of them.

120
00:10:25,838 --> 00:10:27,058
[SPEAKER_01]: The gateway we spoke about.

121
00:10:27,438 --> 00:10:31,579
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that's the gateway, but sometimes you willingly turn a blind eye to the gateway.

122
00:10:31,979 --> 00:10:36,100
[SPEAKER_01]: To the extent you're able to ignore the gateway, to that extent the hindrances will still find a way in.

123
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:46,041
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the chief hindrance, on account of which people then let all the other four in, so to speak, it's sensuality.

124
00:10:47,282 --> 00:10:49,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing the danger in sensuality,

125
00:10:49,988 --> 00:10:52,829
[SPEAKER_01]: That means not seeing the danger in the basis of five hindrances.

126
00:10:53,889 --> 00:11:00,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, when the worry, when the hindrance that you don't want comes, you will not be able to remain surmounting it.

127
00:11:00,630 --> 00:11:01,891
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be involved.

128
00:11:01,911 --> 00:11:06,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your practice up to that point was involvement with the hindrances, by choice.

129
00:11:07,512 --> 00:11:13,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Through welcoming sensuality in your thoughts, delighting it in your thoughts, and above all, not seeing the danger, the peril of it.

130
00:11:14,454 --> 00:11:15,614
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, by the way, where the

131
00:11:17,012 --> 00:11:19,454
[SPEAKER_01]: The crux of that recognition also is the danger.

132
00:11:19,974 --> 00:11:27,921
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger in sensuality is not that later on, you know, things will not work out or it will be too much to manage.

133
00:11:27,981 --> 00:11:30,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, sensuality is not really good.

134
00:11:30,563 --> 00:11:41,893
[SPEAKER_01]: The danger of... even before you engage in sensuality, the danger of not seeing the danger in sensuality is the danger of implicit thought waiting to come with the fifth sensuality.

135
00:11:42,593 --> 00:11:55,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through willingly delighting in sensuality, you're willingly accepting anxiety, worry, fear, threat, sluggishness, lack of energy, depression, everything else that comes through the hindrances your way.

136
00:11:56,522 --> 00:12:01,546
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you recognize that, oh, the danger becomes apparent, that's the immediate danger.

137
00:12:02,364 --> 00:12:06,807
[SPEAKER_01]: In the prospect of sensuality... We're not talking about the results of sensuality are dangerous.

138
00:12:07,407 --> 00:12:10,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Before you engage with sensuality, sensuality is dangerous.

139
00:12:10,669 --> 00:12:15,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, if a monk understands that, or a person understands that, they will not want the sensuality.

140
00:12:15,072 --> 00:12:17,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a simile of the burning embers in the pit.

141
00:12:18,314 --> 00:12:21,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing the danger of it, you enjoy burning yourself with it.

142
00:12:21,596 --> 00:12:27,560
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment you understand the danger, then your mind will be turning everywhere else, except to that pit.

143
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:29,621
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't... that's the only place it doesn't want to be in.

144
00:12:30,992 --> 00:12:47,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing the connection between welcoming sensuality and the implicit weight of the danger that's hidden in that welcoming of all the five that can overwhelm your mind to the point of madness, you realize, oh, no amount of sensuality is worth accepting the risk.

145
00:12:48,347 --> 00:12:49,548
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the danger becomes apparent.

146
00:12:49,568 --> 00:12:55,153
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the simile of like, you know, keeping your head on the chopping block would be basically sensuality.

147
00:12:57,398 --> 00:13:02,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's comfortable, you know, props your neck in the right angle, it relaxes your muscle, it's great, it's very, very agreeable.

148
00:13:03,659 --> 00:13:05,840
[SPEAKER_01]: But it implies a waiting axe to drop.

149
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:08,620
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a chopping block.

150
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:17,882
[SPEAKER_01]: And the axe and the peril that will come with it is basically the other four that are inseparable from the chopping block.

151
00:13:18,242 --> 00:13:23,983
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, again, people don't see a connection between the perils that they are exposed and that they can see

152
00:13:25,068 --> 00:13:28,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And the entrance for those parallels through their acceptance of sensuality.

153
00:13:28,637 --> 00:13:32,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they do, then as they go again, as the Buddha said, the danger will become apparent.

154
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:37,442
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason they don't see that, and they keep engaging in spirituality, is because there is no apparent danger.

155
00:13:38,102 --> 00:13:45,205
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the first jhāna, often the simile of the pleasure of the jhāna, it's just always described in terms of relief from the danger.

156
00:13:45,666 --> 00:13:56,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Like crossing the desert, you don't know whether you survive, then you come out, then you survive, being released from the jail, come back alive, your home even better, paying the debt back, and now you're safe.

157
00:13:56,550 --> 00:13:57,931
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always the sense of safety.

158
00:13:58,755 --> 00:14:04,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense of safety is not having anybody knocking on your door, threatening to you, or experiencing worry, or...

159
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:11,904
[SPEAKER_01]: The sense of safety is not welcoming sensuality, through which no liability of other hindrances can apply to you.

160
00:14:12,345 --> 00:14:21,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And above, like, the reason why this is so great and wholesome, is because you realize it's entirely up to you, welcoming sensuality or not.

161
00:14:21,751 --> 00:14:23,312
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't depend on the circumstances.

162
00:14:23,352 --> 00:14:23,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, you know,

163
00:14:24,611 --> 00:14:27,393
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not lucky, it's just these things coming my way, I can't say no.

164
00:14:27,673 --> 00:14:30,675
[SPEAKER_01]: You can always say no to welcoming sensuality on the level of your thoughts.

165
00:14:30,775 --> 00:14:33,818
[SPEAKER_01]: You have thoughts of sensuality, but what is your attitude?

166
00:14:34,298 --> 00:14:39,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is agreeable, friendly, dear to me, not dangerous, entertaining.

167
00:14:40,142 --> 00:14:46,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, this is the entrance for basically the aeons of saṃsāra and suffering that comes with it.

168
00:14:47,287 --> 00:14:48,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Welcoming of sensuality.

169
00:14:50,450 --> 00:14:56,233
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's when, practically speaking, always, the other four hindrances will torment you.

170
00:14:57,213 --> 00:15:00,455
[SPEAKER_01]: There's only so much you can control in terms of your circumstances and avoid.

171
00:15:00,555 --> 00:15:02,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Sooner or later, things will come up, internally.

172
00:15:03,296 --> 00:15:05,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Worry, anxiety, depression, whatever else.

173
00:15:06,337 --> 00:15:09,839
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is because you do not see the peril of the sensuality.

174
00:15:09,899 --> 00:15:13,160
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the danger of the... You don't see bait as a bait.

175
00:15:14,081 --> 00:15:16,742
[SPEAKER_01]: See, sensuality comes up, you don't recognize the bait of it.

176
00:15:17,082 --> 00:15:18,663
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't see the hook inside of it.

177
00:15:20,332 --> 00:15:21,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the hook is.

178
00:15:21,213 --> 00:15:26,419
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no like, oh yes, you know, like on the level of psychologization, yeah, yeah, sensuality.

179
00:15:27,018 --> 00:15:31,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time I engage with it, you know, it's not really... afterwards you don't feel that great, so it's better.

180
00:15:31,619 --> 00:15:34,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... that's fine, but it's on the level of psychology.

181
00:15:34,160 --> 00:15:35,620
[SPEAKER_01]: They will not uproot things.

182
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:39,901
[SPEAKER_01]: They will just, you know, convince you maybe to restrain a bit more.

183
00:15:40,541 --> 00:15:56,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But for uprooting of sensuality, uprooting of the gateway of the other four hindrances, for uprooting of the domain of the five, you need to see the apparent, inherent, implicit peril waiting before you even engage, not in... after you engage.

184
00:15:57,845 --> 00:16:01,607
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why always sensuality was compared to like a bait, different types of bait.

185
00:16:02,288 --> 00:16:04,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Poisonous drink, that you don't know it's poisonous.

186
00:16:04,729 --> 00:16:07,030
[SPEAKER_01]: The hunter sets up the bait for the deer.

187
00:16:07,050 --> 00:16:09,851
[SPEAKER_01]: He gets trapped because he didn't see the bait.

188
00:16:10,372 --> 00:16:11,892
[SPEAKER_01]: That's on the level of the thoughts.

189
00:16:11,952 --> 00:16:15,034
[SPEAKER_01]: When you have thoughts of sensuality, you need to start seeing them as bait.

190
00:16:15,814 --> 00:16:19,476
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how, then, you will become unable to welcome it.

191
00:16:19,676 --> 00:16:22,017
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just choose to not welcome sensuality.

192
00:16:22,377 --> 00:16:23,078
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

193
00:16:24,069 --> 00:16:30,433
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you contemplate the danger in it, as the Buddha would say, then you realize your welcoming will start to fade.

194
00:16:30,733 --> 00:16:36,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason you used to welcome it, because you never saw what it implies, right there and then, not later.

195
00:16:38,218 --> 00:16:44,482
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the danger becomes apparent, you understand the extent of gratification, and then you understand the extent of escape.

196
00:16:44,922 --> 00:16:47,824
[SPEAKER_01]: You know exactly, oh, if I keep contemplating the danger,

197
00:16:48,450 --> 00:16:52,671
[SPEAKER_01]: The gratification, which is my welcoming and delighting in the prospect of it, will fade.

198
00:16:53,331 --> 00:16:55,252
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will be the thorough escape from this.

199
00:16:57,692 --> 00:17:10,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the abandoning of the hindrances is done through you basically preventing yourself from welcoming certain aspects of those hindrances, because of which you are then subjected to all of them.

200
00:17:11,454 --> 00:17:17,597
[SPEAKER_01]: The effort you make in protecting that one guard doorway, you realize, oh, now you're free from all of it.

201
00:17:17,737 --> 00:17:19,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And that relief is your energy now.

202
00:17:20,019 --> 00:17:20,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Your alertness.

203
00:17:20,739 --> 00:17:21,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything comes out of it.

204
00:17:22,600 --> 00:17:27,243
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to, I'm so tired today, I can't even think straight because I'm overwhelmed by the fatigue.

205
00:17:28,143 --> 00:17:29,363
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm overwhelmed by the worry.

206
00:17:29,724 --> 00:17:38,727
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that overwhelms you, burdens you, hinders you, narrows down your context, your peripheral awareness, doesn't matter how much you practice it.

207
00:17:39,127 --> 00:17:40,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Because... Why?

208
00:17:40,768 --> 00:17:41,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're still liable to it.

209
00:17:42,129 --> 00:17:42,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

210
00:17:42,989 --> 00:17:45,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're still not seeing the peril of central thoughts.

211
00:17:46,550 --> 00:17:50,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And don't confuse seeing the peril of central thoughts with not having central thoughts.

212
00:17:51,563 --> 00:17:52,403
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the same thing.

213
00:17:52,904 --> 00:17:56,025
[SPEAKER_01]: You're gonna have sensual thoughts, or rather thoughts towards sensuality.

214
00:17:56,686 --> 00:17:58,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where you see the peril.

215
00:17:59,187 --> 00:18:04,129
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you have no thoughts of sensuality arising anymore and the significance of sensual joy has disappeared.

216
00:18:04,690 --> 00:18:05,330
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that stays.

217
00:18:05,810 --> 00:18:08,932
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you surmount it, not you prevent it from arising.

218
00:18:12,213 --> 00:18:19,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if you have surmounted it for a long period of time, it will eventually even stop arising on its own, not because you stopped it.

219
00:18:22,987 --> 00:18:36,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, this delighting in sensuality or valuing pleasure, sense pleasures... Well, not seeing the danger in it, implies already value of the pleasant experience that this offers.

220
00:18:36,797 --> 00:18:39,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you...

221
00:18:39,880 --> 00:18:41,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you get anger...

222
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:48,965
[SPEAKER_00]: And getting angry, you know, if you get angry because you're irritated, it's a painful situation.

223
00:18:49,605 --> 00:18:55,390
[SPEAKER_00]: And that prospect of getting angry, getting rid of the displeasure, is... Also a delight.

224
00:18:55,450 --> 00:18:57,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you value the pleasure that comes from it.

225
00:18:58,152 --> 00:19:02,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if people don't want to get angry, even if they say, well, I don't... anger is bad, I don't want to get angry.

226
00:19:03,536 --> 00:19:09,681
[SPEAKER_01]: You will get angry, because mentally, your mind will accept the offer of anger as a hindrance.

227
00:19:10,845 --> 00:19:13,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your mind has been accepting the offer of sensuality.

228
00:19:14,887 --> 00:19:18,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And same, when the doubt arise, you will be unable to remain unmoved in the face of doubt.

229
00:19:19,549 --> 00:19:26,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how much you would wish to be unmoved, your mind will be gripped by the doubt, because that's your mode of being.

230
00:19:26,171 --> 00:19:27,772
[SPEAKER_01]: You've been taking up the hindrances.

231
00:19:28,212 --> 00:19:30,453
[SPEAKER_01]: By taking up one, you're taking up the five.

232
00:19:32,998 --> 00:19:37,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But, like, as you can see, in each hindrance there's this value of pleasure.

233
00:19:37,479 --> 00:19:37,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

234
00:19:38,059 --> 00:19:38,459
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

235
00:19:38,479 --> 00:19:43,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, instead of dealing with this hindrance like this, this is... Start seeing what's in common to any hindrance.

236
00:19:44,141 --> 00:19:49,202
[SPEAKER_01]: The unwholesomeness of it, the implicit value of of getting rid of the discomfort.

237
00:19:49,882 --> 00:19:52,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's another reason why you get pressured into taking it.

238
00:19:52,662 --> 00:19:55,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you get pressured and then you choose to accept the pressure and then you take it.

239
00:19:56,263 --> 00:19:56,583
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep...

240
00:19:56,603 --> 00:19:58,564
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yes, the pressure of sensuality is unpleasant.

241
00:19:58,964 --> 00:19:59,424
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why

242
00:19:59,850 --> 00:20:05,191
[SPEAKER_01]: You take it, because not just you free yourself from that displeasure first, but on top of it you get more pleasure through the sense object.

243
00:20:06,332 --> 00:20:08,672
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you are hungry and you want to eat the mangoes.

244
00:20:09,572 --> 00:20:11,913
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the simile of the deer herd that I often call.

245
00:20:12,193 --> 00:20:13,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The deer are factually hungry.

246
00:20:14,034 --> 00:20:14,934
[SPEAKER_01]: They need to eat.

247
00:20:18,315 --> 00:20:28,097
[SPEAKER_01]: But the difference is whether you learn how to eat everything but the bait, or whether you see no difference, and then either eat everything or try to not eat anything.

248
00:20:29,357 --> 00:20:36,079
[SPEAKER_01]: And either way, of those examples that the Buddha gave, those deer herd were caught by the deer, by the deer hunter.

249
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:40,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I was saying, you don't have to worry about the four hindrances.

250
00:20:41,181 --> 00:20:45,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Just worry about the one that is always the gateway for all the other four, which is sensuality.

251
00:20:48,663 --> 00:20:51,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter, I'm an angry type, I'm a slow type, I'm a sluggish type.

252
00:20:52,224 --> 00:20:53,705
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that means you're a sensual type.

253
00:20:55,450 --> 00:21:08,613
[SPEAKER_01]: You're the type that delights... You're the type that cultivates delight in the manner that then allows all these other hindrances to proliferate, expand, and so on.

254
00:21:26,681 --> 00:21:32,585
[SPEAKER_00]: That delighting in pleasure means that you're going to be not delighting in pain, which means you're going to be going for pleasure.

255
00:21:33,326 --> 00:21:34,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, and it's like...

256
00:21:34,347 --> 00:21:36,428
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get entangled even more and more, yeah.

257
00:21:37,529 --> 00:21:44,274
[SPEAKER_01]: You start seeing the danger in delighting in the prospect of pleasure, that in itself is not painful.

258
00:21:44,634 --> 00:21:47,456
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a neutral, neutral thing.

259
00:21:47,476 --> 00:21:49,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Getting pleasure, not getting pleasure is the level of pain.

260
00:21:51,979 --> 00:21:55,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the danger before you engage with pleasure, it's not painful.

261
00:21:56,435 --> 00:21:57,196
[SPEAKER_01]: It's eye-opening.

262
00:21:58,857 --> 00:22:01,259
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to work your way to that level to see there.

263
00:22:01,779 --> 00:22:06,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for as long as you think the hindrances are the objects of your attention.

264
00:22:06,243 --> 00:22:07,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I got this sensuality now.

265
00:22:07,564 --> 00:22:08,565
[SPEAKER_01]: I got this ill-will now.

266
00:22:09,085 --> 00:22:09,606
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not.

267
00:22:09,986 --> 00:22:11,467
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nowhere near where they're rooted.

268
00:22:11,487 --> 00:22:12,808
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're not going to be dealing with anything.

269
00:22:12,829 --> 00:22:14,210
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just managing and covering it up.

270
00:22:17,052 --> 00:22:21,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what I mean.

271
00:22:21,175 --> 00:22:23,097
[SPEAKER_01]: Existentially, you're always within the hindrances.

272
00:22:25,282 --> 00:22:29,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensory strain prevents you from proliferating further, but your mind is still within it.

273
00:22:32,185 --> 00:22:34,807
[SPEAKER_01]: So, don't think you're safe, because there's no sensuality arising.

274
00:22:38,048 --> 00:22:41,230
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think you are safe, you ask yourself, well, can the non-arisen sensuality arise?

275
00:22:41,270 --> 00:22:42,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I see a connection?

276
00:22:42,170 --> 00:22:45,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I see how that will not affect me, even if it happens?

277
00:22:46,892 --> 00:22:47,533
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no idea.

278
00:22:47,833 --> 00:22:49,614
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have no idea how to escape the five hindrances.

279
00:22:49,714 --> 00:22:52,035
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, here you are practicing meditation for your jhānas.

280
00:22:52,869 --> 00:22:56,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the jhānas are once you surmount the hindrances.

281
00:22:56,951 --> 00:23:04,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You surmount the hindrances, the joys and griefs in regard to the world, anything, basically, that might narrow your context.

282
00:23:04,875 --> 00:23:05,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, abides in that.

283
00:23:05,815 --> 00:23:09,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the pleasure of seclusion of that is what the jhāna comes out of.

284
00:23:10,577 --> 00:23:11,758
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I say, often people think

285
00:23:12,444 --> 00:23:23,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Give me a technique, I'll enter a special experience that I will call jhāna, I will believe it's a jhāna, and then these hindrances will stop arising for me, and joys and griefs of the world will not come my way.

286
00:23:25,073 --> 00:23:25,853
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the other way around.

287
00:23:27,394 --> 00:23:31,897
[SPEAKER_01]: You surmount the five hindrances, you overcome joys and griefs, so that's the work.

288
00:23:32,638 --> 00:23:37,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the safety you get is the pleasure of the jhāna, then you just need to maintain and protect.

289
00:23:39,933 --> 00:23:45,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see why people would like meditation technique, meditation experience, everything else falls off.

290
00:23:45,496 --> 00:23:47,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the implicit, you know, that's where the work is.

291
00:23:48,697 --> 00:23:56,161
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I get the jhāna, my work is done for me, as opposed to I do the work, which is far more unpleasant.

292
00:23:56,921 --> 00:24:00,563
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you cultivate that kind of view, you will get nowhere in practice.

293
00:24:00,583 --> 00:24:02,603
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what special experiences you have.

